\section{Introduction}

A central task in quantitative reasoning under uncertainty is to formalize the
\emph{calculus} by which evidential values are compared, composed, and updated.
In this paper we develop such a calculus at the level of abstract structure by
isolating the algebraic and order-theoretic primitives that underlie many
epistemic uncertainty frameworks. Our guiding idea is to model the
\emph{syntax} of epistemic combination---independently of any particular
semantics---as a symmetric monoidal posetal category and to study its
axiomatizations and transformations categorically. [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)

\paragraph{Epistemic syntax.}
We define an \emph{epistemic syntax} to be a non-trivial unital symmetric
monoidal preorder\footnote{Equivalently, a posetal symmetric monoidal category.}
\((\V,\le_\V,\otimes, \mathbbm{1}_\V)\), whose objects are epistemic values,
whose order encodes comparative strength of support, and whose monoidal product
\(\otimes\) encodes fusion (combination) of evidential values. This abstraction
is motivated by and generalizes concrete systems such as certainty factors (CF),
possibility theory (PT), bipolar possibility (PT$_{\mathrm{bi}}$), and interval
probabilities (IP), all of which we instantiate as examples of epistemic
syntaxes. [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)

\paragraph{Axioms and epistemological principles.}
Within this syntactic layer we formalize several epistemological attitudes as
algebraic/ordering axioms: \emph{optimism} (existence of a top/``truth''
element), \emph{completeness} (all joins), \emph{closure} (existence of an
internal hom), \emph{conservativity} (fusion cannot reduce support),
\emph{idempotency} (repeated self-evidence does not change support),
\emph{fallibilism} (any belief can be weakened by suitable counter-evidence),
and \emph{cancellativity} (fusion preserves order comparisons). We label these
as (E1)–(E8) and analyze their interactions. In particular, we prove: (i) a
no-go result showing that a complete non-trivial syntax cannot be simultaneously
closed, conservative, and cancellative; and (ii) on complete syntaxes,
\emph{closure is equivalent to fallibilism}. We also obtain a structural
uniqueness result: on a totally ordered base with \( \mathbbm{1}_\V=\top \),
idempotency forces the monoidal product to be \(\min\), making PT the unique
idempotent syntax on \([0,1]\) up to order-isomorphism. See
Theorems~\ref{thm:nogo}, \ref{thm:closed-fallible-equiv}, and
\ref{thm:pt-unique}. [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)

\paragraph{Change of syntax via monoidal functors.}
To compare or migrate between uncertainty calculi, we formalize
\emph{conservative} and \emph{liberal} changes of syntax as lax and oplax
symmetric monoidal functors \(F:\V\to\W\). Conservative changes cannot
\emph{create} more certainty by fusion; liberal changes cannot \emph{lose}
certainty by fusion. Balanced changes are strictly monoidal. We show that the
bipolar possibility and interval-probability syntaxes are equivalent via a
balanced change (PT$_{\mathrm{bi}}\simeq$ IP), and we construct natural liberal
maps from PT or PT$_\mathrm{bi}$ to CF. See \S\ref{sec:change-frameworks}. [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)

\paragraph{Enrichment and relational semantics.}
Given a base syntax \(\V\), we incorporate quantitative uncertainty into a
category \(\H\) of hypotheses by enriching \(\H\) in \(\V\) in the sense of
enriched category theory. Thus each hom-object \(\H(H,K)\in\V\) expresses the
context-dependent strength of \(K\) relative to \(H\), and identities/compose
respect \(\mathbbm{1}_\V\) and \(\otimes\). Changes of syntax \(F:\V\to\W\)
induce change-of-base functors \(\Cat_\V\to\Cat_\W\) (Eilenberg–Kelly), yielding
a principled procedure to transport uncertainty assignments across frameworks.
See \S\ref{sec:enrichment}. [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)

\paragraph{Relational updating as internal-hom calculus.}
For closed syntaxes (i.e., those admitting an internal hom \([{-},{-}]\))
we define the \emph{\(\V\)-update} of \(\H\) by an evidence object \(E\in\H\)
as the \(\V\)-category \(\H_E\) with the same objects and
\[
  \H_E(H,K) \;:=\; [{\,\H(H,K)\otimes \H(K,E)\,},\,\H(H,E)].
\]
This expresses the compatibility of the \(H\!\to\!K\) comparison with evidence
\(E\) as an internal-hom residual. The construction is functorial in changes of
syntax. See \S\ref{sec:updating}. [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)

\begin{introthm}
Let \(\V=((0,\infty),\le,\times,1)\) be the likelihood-ratio syntax and let
\(\H\) be a \(\V\)-enriched category of hypotheses with
\(\H(H,K)=\frac{p(K)}{p(H)}\). Then the \(\V\)-update by evidence \(E\) yields
posterior odds:
\[
  \H_E(H,K)=\frac{p(H\mid E)}{p(K\mid E)}.
\]
Equivalently, \(\V\)-updating recovers Bayesian updating (non-normalized), and
normalization can be obtained by reference to a top element. 
\end{introthm}
\noindent
We also show that for PT the same construction recovers Dubois–Prade
possibilistic conditioning, while for CF it coincides with log-odds Bayesian
updating composed with the (bounded) back-projection to CF-values. See
Theorem~\ref{thm:bayes-as-update} and \S\ref{sec:other-updates}. [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)

\paragraph{Contributions.}
Formally, our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(C\arabic*)]
  \item A categorical definition of epistemic syntax and axiomatizations (E1)–(E8),
        together with structural results on their (in)compatibilities and
        equivalences (Theorems~\ref{thm:nogo}, \ref{thm:closed-fallible-equiv},
        \ref{thm:pt-unique}). [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)
  \item A functorial theory of syntax change via (op)lax monoidal functors, and
        balanced equivalence \(\mathrm{PT}_{\mathrm{bi}}\simeq \mathrm{IP}\)
        (Lemma~\ref{lem:ptbi-ip-equivalence}), plus liberal maps to CF.
        [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)
  \item An enriched, relational updating calculus that specializes to Bayesian
        updating on \(((0,\infty),\times)\), to possibilistic conditioning on PT,
        and to CF updating in log-space (Theorem~\ref{thm:bayes-as-update}).
        [1](https://skapende-my.sharepoint.com/personal/torgeir_aambo_ffi_no/Documents/Microsoft%20Copilot%20Chat-filer/a_categorical_formalization_of_epistemic_uncertainty_frameworks.pdf)
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Organization.}
Section~\ref{sec:cats} recalls categorical preliminaries. Section~\ref{sec:syntax}
develops epistemic syntax and axioms. Section~\ref{sec:examples} treats CF, PT,
PT$_{\mathrm{bi}}$, and IP, including the equivalence
PT$_{\mathrm{bi}}\simeq$IP. Section~\ref{sec:change-frameworks} formalizes
syntax changes via (op)lax monoidal functors. Section~\ref{sec:enrichment}
covers enrichment and change of base. Section~\ref{sec:updating} defines
\(\V\)-updating and proves the Bayesian/possibilistic/CF specializations.
